From 3efc665c2d79923f811ad8ad467933a080eb9f75 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 11:06:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] agentPlugins: surface unreadable files in plugin
 discovery

The agent host runs inside the dev container with a file system
provider for the file: scheme only, while a remote window addresses
the same disk as vscode-remote://dev-container+... When such a URI
reached the host's IFileService the read failed with ENOPRO, and the
readJsonFile and pathExists helpers in pluginParsers.ts swallowed every
error as "absent". A file system the host cannot address was therefore
indistinguishable from a file that does not exist, which is why a whole
cluster of namespace bugs (customizations, .mcp.json, CLAUDE.md, hooks
and skills silently reading as missing in a remote window) stayed
invisible for weeks.

The helpers now classify the failure with toFileOperationResult: a
genuinely missing file stays silent (FILE_NOT_FOUND, and also
FILE_NOT_DIRECTORY, since a path under a regular file such as a file
named .claude does not exist either), anything else is logged at warn
level with the resource and the error message, and the helper still
returns "absent" so discovery never crashes. The same swallow pattern
around fileService.resolve in readSkills, readMarkdownComponents,
readInstructionComponents, the Claude rule scan and the native plugin
scan is replaced by a shared tryResolve helper with the same
classification.

The leaf helpers take the logger as a required, nullable parameter so
every caller has to decide. The composite plugin readers that the
workbench also calls take it as an optional trailing parameter and stay
silent when it is omitted, which matches their previous behavior. On
the agent host the logger is threaded from ClaudeAgentSession,
ClaudeAgent, CopilotAgent and CodexAgent into the Claude hook, MCP,
rule, skill and native plugin scans, SessionMcpDiscovery, the folder
picker probe and parsePlugin.

toFileSystemProviderErrorCode alone is not enough for this: FileService
rethrows a missing file from resolve() as a FileOperationError with
FILE_NOT_FOUND and wraps every readFile() error the same way, and a
FileOperationError does not carry the "(FileSystemError)" name marker
that function looks for.
